RWFS Example: Carbon Ion (C⁺)
=============================

This example shows the complete workflow for generating relativistic
radial wavefunctions for the carbon ion (Z = 6, Ne = 5) using RWFS.


1. Input Preparation
--------------------
Edit the input file (e.g. input-carbon_II) with:

  - Atomic number          : 6
  - Number of electrons    : 5
  - Orbitals (spectroscopic notation), for example:
      1s 2s 2p- 2p 3s 3p- 3p 3d- 3d
      4s 4p- 4p 4d- 4d 4f- 4f
      5s 5p- 5p 5d- 5d 5f- 5f 5g- 5g
  - Potential and output options as required


2. Run the Calculation
----------------------
python /path/to/rwfs.py < input-carbon_II > output_carbon_II

This produces:
  radial.rwf          – ASCII radial wavefunctions
  grasp_carbon        – GRASP-compatible ASCII orbitals
  potential_carbon    – potential data (if requested)
  summary.dat         – energies and radial moments


3. Convert ASCII to Binary GRASP Format
---------------------------------------
(a) Compile the converter
    gfortran -O2 -o readrwf readrwf_grasp.f

(b) Convert
    ./readrwf << EOF
    2
    grasp_carbon
    carbon_TF_rwf.w
    EOF

  - 2               selects ASCII → binary conversion
  - grasp_carbon    input ASCII file
  - carbon_TF_rwf.w binary output file (GRASP-compatible)


4. Output Files
---------------
radial.rwf          Full radial wavefunctions (ASCII)
grasp_carbon        GRASP-format orbitals (ASCII)
carbon_TF_rwf.w     Binary GRASP wavefunctions
summary.dat         Orbital energies and radial moments
potential_carbon    Central potential V(r)

All 25 requested orbitals converged successfully.
